# The Math++ grammar

This is the grammar of a `.mpp` file, written as EBNF. A `.mpp` file is a paper: prose with maths in it. The parser has two layers. The **document layer** splits the file into blocks (headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, maths, figures, imports). The **maths layer** parses what sits between `$ … $` and `$$ … $$`.

Notation: `"x"` is literal text, `[ a ]` is optional, `{ a }` is zero or more, `a | b` is a choice, `(* … *)` is a comment. `NL` is a line break and `BLANK` is an empty line.

## 1. Document layer

```
document      = { block } ;
block         = heading | rule | display_math | figure | embed_import
              | verbatim | table | quote | list | footnote_def
              | directive | comment | title_line | references | theorem | paragraph ;

heading       = ("#" | "##" | "###" | "####" | "#####" | "######") " " inline NL ;
rule          = "---" NL ;                                 (* horizontal line *)
display_math  = "$$" math_text "$$" [ " " "(" label_number ")" ] NL ;   (* may span lines *)
figure        = "Figure:" " " figure_spec [ "." " " inline ] NL ;
embed_import  = "![[" path "]]" NL ;                       (* import every name *)
verbatim      = "```" NL { any_line } "```" NL ;           (* shown as-is, never evaluated *)
table         = table_row NL table_rule NL { table_row NL } ;
table_row     = "|" { inline "|" } ;
table_rule    = "|" { ("---" | ":--" | "--:" | ":-:") { "-" } "|" } ;
quote         = { ">" " " inline NL }- ;
list          = { list_item }- ;
list_item     = { "  " } ("- " | "* " | digit { digit } ". ") inline NL ;
footnote_def  = "[^" name "]:" " " inline NL ;
directive     = "%" letter { any_char } NL ;              (* %noeval, %sf 4, %cite …: a word straight after the % (§2.4, §6) *)
comment       = ( "% " { any_char } NL ) { "% " { any_char } NL }
              | "%{" { any_char | NL } "%}" NL ;           (* formatted comment (§6) *)
title_line    = ( "Title:" | "Author:" | "Date:" | "Abstract:" ) " " inline NL ;   (* only before the first real block (§6) *)
references    = "References" [ ":" ] NL { ref_entry NL } | "References from " wiki_link NL ;   (* §6 *)
theorem       = "**" theorem_kind [ " " thm_number ] ( "." "**" [ " (" inline ")" ] [ "." ]
                                                 | "**" ( " (" inline ")" "." | "." ) )
                " " paragraph_text ;                        (* **Lemma 2.**, **Lemma 1** (Scaling)., **Proof** (pairing). (§6.4) *)
theorem_kind  = "Definition" | "Theorem" | "Lemma" | "Corollary"
              | "Proposition" | "Example" | "Remark" | "Proof" | "Conjecture" ;
thm_number    = ( letter | digit ) { letter | digit | "." } ;
paragraph     = paragraph_text ;                           (* lines up to a BLANK *)
paragraph_text= inline { NL inline } ( BLANK | EOF ) ;
```

A paragraph that matches one of the sentence forms in §3 (imports and helpers) is read as that form; any other paragraph is plain prose. A sentence form that fills a whole line is a block of its own even without a blank line around it, and a paragraph that starts `Let $…$` is read as definitions (the maths in it defines variables). `%noeval` marks the maths block right after it. An unclosed `$$`, `` ``` `` fence or `**Proof.**` is reported and recovers at the next blank line, heading or end of file. A `**Proof.**` block ends at the `∎` (or `\qed`) that closes it.

### Inline markup

```
inline        = { inline_item } ;
inline_item   = text | emphasis | strong | inline_math | code | wiki_link
              | link | footnote_ref | eqref | citation | escape ;
code          = "`" { any_char } "`" ;                     (* shown as typed; no markup inside *)
emphasis      = "*" inline "*" ;
strong        = "**" inline "**" ;
inline_math   = "$" math_text "$" ;
wiki_link     = "[[" path [ "|" text ] "]]" ;              (* link to another .mpp file *)
link          = "[" inline "](" url ")" ;
footnote_ref  = "[^" name "]" ;
eqref         = "\eqref{" name "}" ;
escape        = "\" ( "$" | "*" | "[" | "]" | "\" | "|" | "#" | "%" ) ;
citation      = "[@" key { ( ";" | "," ) " " "@" key } "]" ;       (* §6 *)
```

## 2. Maths layer

### 2.1 Statements

Every maths span (inline or display) is one **statement line**, or several lines joined by `\\` (inside `aligned`, an `&` marks where lines line up and is otherwise ignored).

```
math_text     = statement { ( "\\" | asking_gap ) statement } [ label ] ;
asking_gap    = (* after a statement that asks (`= ?`, `≈ ?`), a space such as `\qquad` before the next statement *) ;
label         = "\label{" name "}" ;
statement     = core { "," clause } [ "%noeval" ] ;
core          = definition | query | check | relation_chain | name_list ;
definition    = def_head (":=" | "≡" | "\equiv") expr [ ( "=" | "≈" | "\approx" ) "?" | "=" ] ;
def_head      = name [ "(" name { "," name } ")" ]         (* f(x, y) := … *)
              | name "_{" name "+1}"                       (* recurrence: a_{n+1} := … *)
              | name "_" integer ;                         (* initial term: a_1 := 1; plain variable v_0 if no recurrence for v *)
query         = expr ( "=" | "≈" | "\approx" ) "?" [ "!" ]
              | expr "=" ;                                 (* an = that ends the line: the same as = ? *)
check         = expr ( "\overset{?}{=}" | "≟" | "?=" | "==" ) expr ;
relation_chain= expr { relation expr } ;
name_list     = name { "," name } ;                        (* only inside Using … from *)
clause        = name "=" expr                              (* where-binding *)
              | name { "," name } "\in" set_expr           (* range, slider *)
              | relation_chain ;                           (* condition, e.g. x > 0 *)
```

- `:=` makes a changeable variable or a function, `≡` makes a constant. A head with arguments defines a function; a bare name with an expression that mentions other names is a no-argument function that recomputes when they change.
- `= ?` asks for the exact answer, `≈ ?` for a number, and `= ?!` for the answer with its working (steps). An `=` that ends a line asks too, without the `?` (`1.321321312 + 5 =`, `E := 2x + 5 =`); the editor shows the answer in the line's answer slot.
- On a display maths line with no clauses, `x = 5` with a single name on the left defines `x` when `x` has no value yet and the right side has one (the way coders and calculators write it); when `x` is already a variable it is a check (✓ or ✗), like `x == 5` and `x \overset{?}{=} 5`. An equation with unknowns on the right (`y = x^2 + 1`), maths inside a sentence and documents with proofs keep the old reading: a statement.
- A definition can ask for its value straight away: `T_0 := 2\pi\sqrt{L/g} \approx ?`. Several statements that ask can share a line, separated by a space such as `\qquad`: `a \approx ? \qquad b \approx ?`.
- A differential equation written as a statement with its conditions (`\frac{dN}{dt} = -\lambda N, \quad N(0) = N_0`) defines its unknown function when the conditions determine it and the document goes on to call the function with an argument (`N(10\,000\,\mathrm{yr})`); a name met only inside differential equations stays the unknown of each.
- Numbers: groups of three digits joined by a thin space are one number (`10\,000`); `5\%` is the number 5/100; `\mathbf 1[A]` is the indicator of the condition $A$ (1 where it holds, 0 elsewhere).
- `name ≡ expr` is a constant definition, except when the right side holds `\pmod`: `a \equiv b \pmod{n}` stays a congruence.
- A name with a number subscript on the left of `:=` (`v_0 := 20`) is an initial term when a recurrence for the same letter exists (`v_{n+1} := …`); otherwise it is the plain variable `v_0`. The parser marks it as an initial term and the evaluator decides.
- A where-binding after a comma holds only for that statement: `$$ A = \pi r^2, \quad r = 3 $$`. Spacing commands (`\quad`, `\;`, `\,`, `\ `) are ignored by the parser.
- A display block that is a bare expression, or an equation with an unknown, is evaluated automatically (preview only), unless it is a definition, a check, or marked `%noeval`.

### 2.2 Expressions

Precedence, lowest first:

| Level | Operators | Associativity |
|---|---|---|
| relation | `= ≠ < > ≤ ≥ ≈ ≡ ∼ ≅ ∝ ≪ ≫ ∈ ∉ ⊂ ⊆ ⇒ ⇔ →`, and `:` (as in `f: ℝ → ℝ`) | chain |
| logic | `∨`, then `∧`, then `¬` (prefix) | left |
| additive | `+ − ± ∓ ∪ ∖` | left |
| multiplicative | `· × ÷ / ∘ ⊗ ⊕ ∩`, implicit product | left |
| unary | prefix `−`, `+`, `±` | prefix |
| big operator | `∑ ∏ ∫ ∬ ∭ ∮ lim`, `d/dx`, `∂` | takes the term to its right (`∇` takes one factor) |
| power | `^`, `_`, primes `′ ″`, `!`, Unicode `²³` | right (for `^`) |
| atom | number, name, group, call, fraction, root, matrix … | |

```
expr          = logic_or ;
logic_or      = logic_and { ("∨" | "\lor") logic_and } ;
logic_and     = logic_not { ("∧" | "\land") logic_not } ;
logic_not     = [ "¬" | "\neg" ] additive ;
additive      = [ sign ] term { add_op term } ;
term          = factor { [ mul_op ] factor } ;             (* no operator = implicit product *)
factor        = big_op | postfix ;
postfix       = atom { "^" script | "_" script | prime | "!" | unicode_super } ;
script        = "{" expr "}" | [ sign ] atom_no_postfix   (* x^2, x^{n+1}, a_n, x^-1 *)
              | "(" expr ")" ;                             (* x^(n+1): the brackets only group *)
                                                           (* x_{max}: two or more letters in braces after _ are an upright label *)
                                                           (* plain a^b^c is a^(b^c); x^23 is x^{23} *)
prime         = "'" | "′" | "″" ;

big_op        = sum_like | integral | limit | derivative | nabla_op | named_op ;
sum_like      = ("\sum" | "∑" | "sum" | "\prod" | "∏" | "prod")
                [ "_" bound_index ] [ "^" script ] term ;
bound_index   = "{" name "=" expr "}" | "(" name "=" expr ")" ;
integral      = int_sign [ "_" script ] [ "^" script ] expr_until_d [ "\cdot" ] differential
              | int_sign [ "_" script ] [ "^" script ] differential term ;  (* \int dx \, f(x) *)
                                                           (* \int \frac{dx}{x} reads the dx in the numerator as the differential *)
int_sign      = "\int" | "∫" | "int" | "\iint" | "∬" | "\iiint" | "∭"
              | "\oint" | "∮" | "\oiint" | "∯" ;
differential  = [ "\," ] "d" name { [ "\," ] "d" name }    (* dx, dy dx, dA, dV, d\mathbf r *)
limit         = ("\lim" | "lim") "_" ( "{" | "(" ) name ("\to" | "→" | "->") expr [ side ] ( "}" | ")" ) term ;
side          = "^+" | "^-" | "^{+}" | "^{-}" | "⁺" | "⁻" | "+" | "-" ;   (* x \to 0^+, plain x->0+ *)
derivative    = "\frac{d" [ "^" integer ] "}{d" name [ "^" integer ] "}" term
              | "\frac{d" name "}{d" name "}"              (* dy/dx as a whole *)
              | "\frac{\partial" [ "^" integer ] [ name ] "}{" partial_dens "}" [ term ]
              | ("\partial_" | "D_") script term
              | "d/d" name term ;
nabla_op      = ("\nabla" | "∇" | "nabla") [ "^2" | "²" ] [ ("\cdot" | "·" | "\times" | "×") ] factor ;
                                                           (* a factor, so \nabla f \cdot \mathbf u is (∇f)·u *)
named_op      = "\operatorname{" name "}" [ "_" script ] [ "^" script ] ( call_args | term ) ;

atom          = number | constant | name_or_call | group | abs | norm | floor | ceil
              | frac | sqrt | binom | matrix | cases | set_lit | interval
              | accent | font_name | text | mathbb ;
number        = digit { digit } [ "." digit { digit } ] [ ("e" | "E") [ "-" ] digit { digit } ] ;
constant      = "\pi" | "π" | "pi" | "e" | "i" | "\infty" | "∞" | "inf" ;
                                                           (* plain inf is ∞; \inf with a backslash is the infimum *)
                                                           (* e and i are names where something binds them: \sum_{i=1}, f(i) := …, subscripts *)
name_or_call  = name [ call_args ] ;                       (* f(x): call if f is a function, else f·(x) *)
call_args     = ( "(" | "\left(" ) expr { "," expr } ( ")" | "\right)" ) ;
name          = letter [ "_" script ] | greek | func_name | "\operatorname{" letters "}"
              | "\mathrm{" letters "}" | accent ;
group         = "(" expr ")" | "[" expr "]" | "{" expr "}" | "\left" delim expr "\right" delim ;
abs           = "|" expr "|" | "\left|" expr "\right|" | "\abs{" expr "}" ;
norm          = "\|" expr "\|" | "‖" expr "‖" ;
floor         = "\lfloor" expr "\rfloor" | "⌊" expr "⌋" | "floor(" expr ")" ;
ceil          = "\lceil" expr "\rceil" | "⌈" expr "⌉" | "ceil(" expr ")" ;
frac          = ( "\frac" | "\tfrac" | "\dfrac" ) "{" expr "}" "{" expr "}"
              | "\tfrac12" ;                               (* digit shorthand: \frac12 = 1/2 *)
sqrt          = "\sqrt" [ "[" expr "]" ] "{" expr "}" | "√" atom | "sqrt(" expr ")"
              | "root(" expr "," expr ")" ;                 (* root(n, x) is the n-th root of x *)
binom         = "\binom{" expr "}{" expr "}" | "binom(" expr "," expr ")" ;
matrix        = "\begin{" mat_env "}" row { "\\" row } "\end{" mat_env "}" ;
mat_env       = "pmatrix" | "bmatrix" | "vmatrix" | "matrix" ;   (* vmatrix = determinant *)
row           = expr { "&" expr } ;
cases         = "\begin{cases}" case_row { "\\" case_row } "\end{cases}" ;
case_row      = expr "&" ( relation_chain | "\text{otherwise}" | "otherwise" ) ;
set_lit       = "\{" [ expr { "," expr } ] "\}" | "∅" | "\emptyset" ;
set_builder   = "\{" name [ "\in" set ] ( ":" | "\mid" ) relation_chain "\}" ;   (* {x ∈ ℝ : x^2 < 4} *)
interval      = ( "[" | "(" ) expr "," expr ( "]" | ")" ) ;
accent        = ( "\hat" | "\bar" | "\vec" | "\dot" | "\ddot" | "\tilde" ) "{" name "}" ;
font_name     = ( "\mathbf" | "\mathcal" | "\mathfrak" ) "{" letters "}" ;
mathbb        = "\mathbb{" ("N" | "Z" | "Q" | "R" | "C") "}" | "ℕ" | "ℤ" | "ℚ" | "ℝ" | "ℂ" ;
text          = "\text{" any "}" ;
```

**The three input forms.** LaTeX (`\frac{a}{b}`, `\alpha`, `\int_0^1`), the plain form (`a/b`, `alpha`, `sqrt(x)`, `sum_(n=1)^inf`, `int_0^1`, `lim_(x->0)`, `<=`, `>=`, `!=`, `->`) and Unicode typed directly (`α`, `∫`, `∑`, `≤`, `x²`) all produce the same tree. Every name in the symbol table (`alpha`, `Omega`, `nabla`, `infty`, `partial`, `in`, `forall` …) means its symbol, with or without the backslash.

**Names.** A run of letters that is a known name (a Greek letter, a symbol, or a function such as `sin`, `ln`, `exp`, `det`) is that name. Any other run of letters is a product of single letters, so `xy` is $x y$. Longer names are written `\operatorname{name}` or `\mathrm{name}`, or get a subscript (`v_0`, `x_{max}`). Precomposed Unicode letters with accents (`ẋ`, `â`, `ȳ`) mean the letter with that accent, and styled letters (`ℝ`, `𝐅`, `ℒ`) mean the letter in that font.

**Calls against products.** `f(x)` is parsed as a call node. The evaluator decides: if `f` is a function it is applied, otherwise it is $f \cdot x$. A known function written without brackets takes the product to its right up to the next function name, `+` or `−`: `\sin 2x` is $\sin(2x)$, and `\sin x \cos x` is $\sin(x)\cos(x)$. Powers on a function name apply to the result: `\sin^2 x` is $(\sin x)^2$, except `^{-1}`, which means the inverse function.

**Integral bodies** run up to their differential: in `\int_0^1 x^2 + 1 \, dx` the body is $x^2 + 1$. A `\sum`, `\prod` or `\lim` takes the multiplicative term to its right, so it stops at the next `+` or `−` (use brackets to take more).

**Remainders.** `a \bmod n` (or `a \mod n`) is the remainder, at the level of `·`; `a \equiv b \pmod{n}` stays a congruence.

### 2.3 Units

A quantity is a number followed by a unit: `9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}`, `9.81 m/s^2`, `100\,\mathrm{km/h}`, `25\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}`, `30^\circ`, `1.5 \times 10^{3}\,\mathrm{m}`.

```
quantity      = ( number | "10^" script ) [ separator ] unit ;
separator     = " " | "\," | "\;" | "\:" | "\ " | "~" ;
unit          = roman_unit { ( "\," | "\cdot" | "·" | "/" ) roman_unit }
              | degrees | plain_unit ;
roman_unit    = ( "\mathrm{" | "\text{" | "\textrm{" ) unit_text "}" [ unit_power ] ;
degrees       = ( "^\circ" | "^{\circ}" | "{}^\circ" | "°" ) [ "C" | "F" | "\mathrm{C}" | "\mathrm{F}" ]
              | "℃" | "℉" ;
plain_unit    = unit_text ;                                (* no spaces inside *)
unit_text     = unit_product { "/" unit_product } ;        (* a / b c means a / (b·c) *)
unit_product  = unit_factor { ( "·" | "\cdot" | "\," | " " ) unit_factor } ;   (* spaces only in \mathrm{} *)
unit_factor   = ( "(" unit_text ")" | unit_name | "1" ) [ unit_power ] ;
unit_power    = "^" ( [ "-" ] integer | "{" [ "-" ] integer [ "/" integer ] "}" | "(" … ")" ) | superscripts ;
unit_name     = [ si_prefix ] symbol ;                     (* km, µs, kΩ; symbols from the unit table *)
conversion    = expr ( "\operatorname{in}" | "\text{in}" | "in" ) unit ;
```

- **No clashes.** A letter is read as a unit only (1) straight after a number, (2) inside `\mathrm{}` after a number or as a conversion target, or (3) when it comes from the Units picker (which writes `\mathrm{…}`). Anywhere else `m` is the variable `m`. So `m \equiv 70\,\mathrm{kg}` and `W := m g` work as expected.
- **Plain units after a number** need a space (`3 m`) unless they can't be a single letter (`2kg`, `10min`, `9.81m/s^2`), so `2x` and `5t^2` stay products. A plain one-letter unit that is a name in scope at that point (a function parameter, an index, an integration variable, a defined variable) is that name: with `f(t) := 3 t^2`, `t` is the parameter, and with `g := 9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}`, `2 g` is twice gravity, not two grams. Write `\mathrm{}` to force the unit. The words `in`, `sec`, `hr`, `deg` and `l` are never plain units, and a unit can't be followed by `(` (so `2 \min(a, b)` is a function).
- `30^\circ` is 30 degrees; `{}^\circ\mathrm{C}` and `^\circ C` are degrees Celsius.
- **Conversion** (`v \operatorname{in} \mathrm{km/h}`, or the prose form `v in km/h`) binds more loosely than `+` and `·`, so `v t \operatorname{in} \mathrm{m}` converts the product. After a bare `in`, a one-letter plain unit is not enough (`x in S` is membership); write `\operatorname{in}` or `\mathrm{}` for those.

### 2.4 Number display

A `%` line whose first character after the `%` is a letter and whose first word is one of the keywords below is a display directive; `% sf 4` (a space first) and any other `%` line stay comments. Directives change how results are shown, never what they are.

```
display     = "%" [ "block" " " ] option NL ;
option      = "sf" " " digits            (* significant figures, 1 to 50; trailing zeros kept *)
            | "dp" " " digits            (* decimal places, 0 to 50; trailing zeros kept *)
            | "sci" [ " " digits ]       (* scientific notation, from 10^n up and below 10^-n; default 4 and -3 *)
            | "eng" [ " " digits ]       (* engineering notation, exponents in steps of 3, same thresholds *)
            | "exact" | "decimal"        (* exact values stay exact (default) | every number as a decimal *)
            | "mixed" | "improper"       (* 3 1/2 | 7/2 (default) *)
            | "deg" | "rad"              (* angle results in degrees | radians (default) *)
            | "polar" | "rect"           (* r∠θ | a + bi (default) *)
            | "plain" ;                  (* no forced notation, ten significant figures again *)
```

- **Scope.** A directive applies from its line to the end of the file (so one at the top is file-wide) and later directives override earlier ones. `%block sf 3` applies only to the next block that has results; any number of `%block` lines before a block all apply to it.- **What each option shows.** Exact numbers stay fractions under `%exact`, whatever `%sf` or `%dp` say; those apply to decimals (approximations, `≈ ?` answers, and every number under `%decimal`). Under `%decimal` whole numbers stay whole unless `%sf`/`%dp` is set. `%sci` and `%eng` also rewrite exact integers outside the thresholds, keeping every digit unless `%sf`/`%dp` is set. Without them a decimal switches to powers of ten from 10^21 up and below 10^-7.
- **Notation.** Plain text shows `6.02 × 10²³`; LaTeX shows `6.02 \times 10^{23}`. `6.02e23` is never shown.
- **Angles.** `%deg` shows an answer in degrees when it is exactly one inverse trig call (`\arcsin`, `\arccos`, `\arctan`, `\operatorname{atan2}`, `\arg`, `\sin^{-1}`…): `\arcsin(1/2) = ?` reads `30°`. It also gives the angle of a polar complex number in degrees. It never changes how inputs are read: `30^\circ` and `30°` are always degrees and a bare `\sin(30)` is always the sine of 30 radians.
- **Complex numbers.** `%polar` shows `5∠0.927295218` in text and `5 e^{0.927295218 i}` in LaTeX (`5∠53.13°` and `5\angle 53.13^\circ` with `%deg`); the magnitude and angle are decimals.
- **Units** follow the same rules: the number in front of the unit is formatted, the unit is untouched.
- **Errors.** A directive that names an option but is malformed (`%sf`, `%sf x`, `%dp 99`, `%exact now`, `%block`) is an error on its own line; the rest of the file runs normally.

## 3. Sentence forms

These prose sentences are read as statements. A sentence may end with `.` and the words are not case-sensitive.

```
using         = "Using" " " "$" name_list "$" [ " as " "$" name_list "$" ] " from " wiki_link "." ;
helper        = verb " " helper_name " of " "$" expr "$" [ " on " "$" interval "$" ]
                [ " at " "$" clause "$" ] "." ;
verb          = "Find" | "Compute" | "Determine" ;
helper_name   = "the domain" | "the range" | "the intercepts" | "the critical points"
              | "the extrema" | "the local extrema" | "the absolute extrema"
              | "the intervals of increase" | "the concavity" | "the inflection points"
              | "the asymptotes" | "the tangent line" | "the normal line"
              | "the linearisation" | "the linearization" ;
analyse       = ("Analyse" | "Analyze") " " "$" expr "$" [ " on " "$" interval "$" ] "." ;
solve         = "Solve" " " "$" equations "$" [ " for " "$" name_list "$" ] "." ;
equations     = expr { "," expr } | cases ;                (* several equations are a system *)
data          = "Data:" " " "$" name "$" " from " wiki_link "." ;   (* a CSV file, relative to the document *)
assume        = "Assume" [ " that" ] " " "$" condition "$" { ( "," [ " and" ] | " and" ) " " "$" condition "$" } "." ;
condition     = relation_chain | name "\in" ( mathbb | interval ) ;   (* a bound on one name *)
```

**Data.** A table whose header cells are each a name in maths, optionally followed by a unit in brackets (`| $t$ (s) | $x$ [m] |`), and whose cells are numbers, defines one data column per header. `Data: $T$ from [[file.csv]].` reads a CSV file the same way: a header row of names with optional units (`t (s), x (m)`; commas, semicolons or tabs), then rows of numbers; it also names the whole table `T` (a matrix), and the file is read again when it changes. For a column `x`, `\bar{x}` is its mean, `x_i` its entry `i` (from 1) and `\sum_i x_i` runs over all its entries, unless those decorated names are defined on their own.

**Assumptions** hold from the sentence to the end of the document (and in files that import it), like definitions. A condition in round brackets after a query or check, `\sqrt{y^2} = ? \quad (y < 0)`, holds for that display block only. Each condition bounds one name: `x > 0`, `0 < x \le 1`, `2x - 1 \ge 0`, `a \neq 0`, `n \in \mathbb{Z}`, `n \in \mathbb{N}`, `x \in [0, 1]`, `x \in \mathbb{R}`. Conditions that contradict the ones in force are an error, and so is assuming something about a name that already has a value.

Every helper has an operator form that means the same thing: `\operatorname{crit} f = ?`, `\operatorname{extrema}_{[0,4]} f = ?`, `\operatorname{tangent}_{x=1} f = ?`, `\operatorname{solve}_x (x^2 = 4) = ?`.

## 4. Figures

```
figure_spec   = fig_item { "," " " fig_item } ;
fig_item      = [ fig_words ] "$" expr "$" [ fig_words ]   (* curve, surface or equation *)
              | "$" name { "," name } "\in" interval "$"    (* plotting range *)
              | fig_keyword [ " " "$" clause "$" ] ;
fig_keyword   = "polar" | "parametric" | "implicit" | "points" | "contour" | "slope field"
              | "vector field" | "gradient field" | "tangent at" | "tangent plane at"
              | "riemann left" | "riemann right" | "riemann mid" | "riemann trapezoid" | "area between"
              | "revolve about" | "taylor" | "newton from"
              | "scatter" | "histogram" | "box plot" ;
```

Words between the items describe them and draw nothing (`fig_words`: any words up to the next `$`, `,` or `. `): `Figure: $\theta(t)$ and the small-angle solution $\theta_0 \cos t$, $t \in [0, 10\,\mathrm s]$.`

`Figure: $y(x)$, $x \in [0, R]$.` draws a curve. An item with two free variables, or a range for both $x$ and $y$, makes a 3D surface: `Figure: $z = x^2 - y^2$, $x, y \in [-2, 2]$.` The text after the first `. ` is the caption.

The argument of a keyword is a list inside one `$…$`, separated by commas outside brackets: `riemann left $n = 8, [0, 2]$`, `slope field $y' = x - y, (0, 1)$`. The calculus keywords add to the curve `y = f(x)` written in the same figure: `tangent at $x = 1$` (a slider moves the point), `riemann left|right|mid|trapezoid $n = 8$` (a slider for the strips; a bound `[a, b]` is optional), `area between` (the first two curves; bounds `[a, b]` optional, else the region they enclose), `taylor $n = 3, a = 1$` (degree and centre) and `newton from $x_0 = 2, n = 4$`. `revolve about $y = 0$` turns the curves into a solid (disks, washers or shells by the axis). `tangent plane at $(1, 1)$` goes with a surface. Fields: `slope field $y' = x - y, (0, 1)$` (starting points are optional), `slope field $x' = y, y' = -x$` (a phase portrait), `vector field $(-y, x)$`, `gradient field $x^2 + y^2$`; without an argument the figure's own item is the field, as in `$y - x$, slope field`. Data: `scatter $t, x$`, `histogram $x$` or `histogram $x, 6$`, `box plot $x, y$`, naming columns of a data table.

## 5. Errors

A maths error is reported at the exact span it comes from, says what was expected ("expected the upper bound after `^`"), and the parser carries on at the next block, so one bad line never stops the rest of the document. Unclosed `$$` closes at the next blank line, with an error. Inside a maths span the parser recovers at the next `,` or line break (`\\`), putting an error placeholder where something was missing, so it always returns a tree. Nesting deeper than 256 brackets, or more than 10 000 operators in a row on one line, is reported as an error instead of being followed; anything up to that is read in full, without running out of stack.

## 6. Comments and paper features

Everything here is optional. A file that uses none of it reads and renders exactly as before.

### 6.1 Formatted comments

```
comment       = line_comment | block_comment ;
line_comment  = { ( "%" | "%" " " ) { any_char } NL }- ;   (* consecutive lines are one comment *)
block_comment = "%{" { any_char | NL } "%}" ;              (* may start and end mid-line *)
```

`% text` is a one-line comment and `%{ … %}` a block comment. A `%` followed straight away by a word (`%noeval`, `%sf 4`, `%cite author-year`) stays a directive; a comment line has a space (or nothing) after the `%`. The text of a comment is read like the rest of the file (headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, **bold**, *italic*, links, `$…$` and `$$…$$` maths), so it can hold anything a block can, but it is only ever shown: its maths is typeset and never evaluated, no definition, assumption, import, label or `%noeval` inside it takes effect, it takes no equation number, and errors inside it are not reported. A comment sits between blocks, or right after a display equation on the same line (`$$ x = 1 $$ % because …`) or the next. An unclosed `%{` is an error and the comment runs to the end of the file. A comment holding nothing but display maths is maths commented out.

### 6.2 Title block, citations, references

```
title_line    = ( "Title:" | "Author:" | "Date:" | "Abstract:" ) " " inline NL ;
citation      = "[@" key { ( ";" | "," ) " "* "@" key } "]" ;
references    = "References" [ ":" ] NL { ref_entry NL }
              | "References from [[" path "]]" ;
ref_entry     = "[@" key "]" [ ":" ] " " author " | " title [ " | " year ] [ " | " link ] ;
key           = { letter | digit | "_" | "-" | ":" | "." | "/" }- ;
```

`Title:`, `Author:` (one line each, repeatable), `Date:` and `Abstract:` (runs to the next blank line) are a title block only when they come before the first real block of the file (directives and comments may come first); later they are ordinary prose. A citation `[@key]`, `[@a; @b]` is checked against the entries written in the file's `References` block; an unknown key, a key defined twice and a malformed entry are errors. `References from [[refs.mpp]]` names a file whose `[@key] Author | Title | Year | link` lines are the entries (every other line of that file is ignored); because the file is read by the caller, the keys are then checked with `citation_diagnostics(doc, entries)`. A `[@` that is not a well-formed citation is plain text.

### 6.3 Directives of the paper features

- `%cite numbered` (the default): citations read `[1]`, numbered in order of first citation, and the list is in that order, uncited entries last. `%cite author-year`: citations read `(Knuth 1984)` (`(Smith and Lee 2001)`, `(Walker et al. 2014)`), and the list is alphabetical. Authors are separated by ` and ` or `;`, and a surname is the part before the first comma, or the last word.
- `%number sections`: headings are numbered (`1`, `1.1`) and theorem-style blocks are numbered per section (`Theorem 2.1`, counting from 1 in each top-level section; `Remark` and `Proof` are not numbered). When the file has exactly one `#` heading and deeper ones, the `#` is the title and the `##` headings are the sections. Without it theorems are numbered in one sequence (`Theorem 1`, `Theorem 2`).
- Proofs always end with `∎`, written or added. Footnotes are numbered superscripts with the notes collected at the end. Equations that have a `\label` are numbered and `\eqref` links to them.

### 6.4 Proofs written as steps

A proof that the checker can follow is a numbered list inside a `**Proof.**` block. Each step is a claim, then a long dash and its reason. A heading may carry a number and a title in brackets: `**Theorem 2** (Euclid).`, `**Lemma 2.**`, `**Proof** (by induction on $n$).`. A `**Conjecture 3.**` block states something not yet proved; a proof after it is checked like any other.

```
proof_steps   = { step_item }- ;                           (* an ordered list inside the proof *)
step_item     = digit { digit } ". " [ step_label ] claim [ reason ] { note } NL { "  " step_item } ;
step_label    = "Base case:" | "Inductive step:" | "Case " inline ":" ;
claim         = inline ;                                   (* prose and $…$ maths: the relations in it are what gets checked *)
reason        = ( " — " | " -- " ) ( "by " reason_text | note ) [ "." ] ;
reason_text   = "assumption" [ " (" any ")" ] | "hypothesis" | "construction" | "definition"
              | "contradiction" | "induction" [ " on " inline ] | "cases"
              | ref { ( " and " | ", " ) ( ref | rule ) }
              | rule { ( " and " | ", " ) ( ref | rule ) } ;
ref           = ( "step" | "steps" ) " " step_list
              | ( "Lemma" | "Theorem" | "Corollary" | "Proposition" | "Conjecture" ) " " thm_number
              | eqref ;
step_list     = range { ( ", " | " and " ) range } ;
range         = integer [ ( "–" | "-" ) integer ] ;
rule          = inline ;                                   (* the algebra, the chain rule, the binomial theorem … *)
note          = "*(gap: " inline ")*"                      (* not yet justified *)
              | "*(cited: " inline citation ")*"            (* an established result from the literature *)
              | "*(external: " citation ")*" ;              (* a claim made elsewhere that nobody here has verified *)
```

- A note may stand alone after the dash (`— *(cited: … [@key])*`) or follow a reason (`— by the divergence theorem *(gap: decay at infinity)*`); a gap note always wins over the reason.
- In a claim, maths after "if", "suppose", "assume" or "let" is a hypothesis of the step, not something it claims. A step whose reason is `by assumption`, `by construction` or `by definition` introduces everything in it.
- A cited or external note must name its source with a citation; the key must have an entry in the file's references (a `References` block, `References from [[file]]`, or a list of `- [@key] …` items under a heading "References").
- The ∎ (or `\qed`) that closes the proof may sit on the line after the last step.
- Statuses (✓ ≈ ⊢ ◇ 📖 ⚠ ✗) and the theorem's summary are computed by the checker and shown in the equation view only; they are never written into the file.
